docs-title = Documentation
docs-intro = Everything about how Mitsuzo works: the cryptography, the API, and running your own server.
docs-security-title = Security Model
docs-security-desc = The complete key hierarchy behind envelope encryption — the short version of what the server actually sees.
docs-security-item1 = Content key (CEK): random 32 bytes per paste, encrypts every data chunk
docs-security-item2 = Key-encryption key: KEK = HKDF-SHA256(Argon2id(password, salt), "mitsuzo-encryption-key")
docs-security-item3 = CEK is wrapped with ChaCha20-Poly1305(KEK, wrap_nonce) into a 48-byte wrapped key, stored server-side
docs-security-item4 = Password change: unwrap CEK with the old KEK, re-wrap with the new KEK — the ciphertext is never touched
docs-security-item5 = Validation: HMAC-SHA256(HKDF(master, "mitsuzo-validation-key"), salt), compared in constant time by the server
docs-security-item6 = Burn receipt: HKDF-SHA256(CEK, "mitsuzo-burn-receipt") — derived from the content key, so it stays valid across password changes
docs-security-item7 = Legacy pastes created before envelope encryption decrypt via the old direct derived-key path
docs-api-title = API Reference
docs-api-desc = All bodies are bitcode-encoded binary. Content endpoints require X-Password-Hash: base64(HMAC-SHA256(validation_key, salt)); failed attempts decrement the paste try count.
docs-api-init = Initialize a paste — body: CreatePasteHeader, response contains the new paste id
docs-api-chunk-put = Upload one 16 MB upload-chunk of ciphertext
docs-api-chunks = Received upload-chunk count (resumable uploads)
docs-api-complete = Finalize a paste upload
docs-api-salt = Bootstrap only: the Argon2id salt (required to derive the password hash) — no paste metadata is exposed here
docs-api-data = Download ciphertext — X-Password-Hash required, Range supported; response is [metadata frame (nonce, file info, wrapped key, try count)] + ciphertext
docs-api-get = Download framed GetPasteHeader + ciphertext
docs-api-password = Change password — body: new salt/wrapped key/hash, X-Password-Hash carries the old hash
docs-api-burn = Burn-after-read receipt — deletes the paste on match
docs-api-stats = Anonymous usage counters
docs-selfhost-title = Self-Hosting
docs-selfhost-desc = Run your own Mitsuzo instance with Docker or from source. The server stores only ciphertext, wrapped keys, and metadata.
docs-selfhost-docker = Docker
docs-selfhost-env = Environment Variables
docs-env-ttl-desc = Maximum paste TTL in seconds
docs-env-size-desc = Maximum paste/file size in bytes
docs-env-demo-desc = Enable demo mode (1, true, yes, on)
docs-selfhost-build = Build from Source
docs-selfhost-more = CLI configuration: MITSUZO_BASE_URL environment variable or config.yml in the OS config directory, /etc/mitsuzo, or the runtime directory.
e2e-encryption = End-to-End Encryption
e2e-desc = Pastes are encrypted in your browser before upload. Your data is encrypted with a random per-paste content key (CEK), which is itself wrapped with a key derived from your password — so the password never directly touches your content and can be changed without re-encrypting. Keys never leave your device.
e2e-item1 = Argon2id(password, salt) → 32-byte master key (19456 KiB memory, 2 iterations)
e2e-item2 = HKDF-SHA256(master, "mitsuzo-encryption-key") → 32-byte key-encryption key (KEK)
e2e-item3 = HKDF-SHA256(master, "mitsuzo-validation-key") → 32-byte validation key
e2e-item4 = Random 32-byte content key (CEK) per paste, wrapped with ChaCha20-Poly1305(KEK)
e2e-item5 = ChaCha20-Poly1305(CEK, derived_nonce, plaintext) → ciphertext; files >64KB split into 64KB chunks with unique derived nonces
e2e-item6 = Unique 16-byte salt, 12-byte base nonce and 12-byte wrap nonce per paste
e2e-item7 = Server never sees your password, plaintext, or unwrapped CEK
zk-validation = Zero-Knowledge Password Validation
zk-desc = The server validates your password without ever knowing it. Here's how:
zk-step1 = Argon2id + HKDF derive the KEK and validation key from your password
zk-step2 = Browser computes HMAC-SHA256(validation_key, salt) and sends this hash to the server
zk-step3 = Server stores only this hash — never the key, salt, or password
zk-step4 = When viewing, browser re-derives keys and hash, sending it in the X-Password-Hash header
zk-step5 = Server compares hashes in constant time (XOR-based) — a match proves the password is correct without revealing anything
zk-note = Even a compromised server cannot decrypt pastes or recover passwords.
workflow-diagram = Workflow Diagram
self-destructing = Self-Destructing Pastes
self-destruct-desc = You control how long your paste lives. Set a time-to-live (TTL) and optionally a maximum number of views.
sd-item1 = Automatic deletion after TTL expires (max 12 hours)
sd-item2 = Optional try-count limit for extra security (1-100 attempts)
sd-item3 = Paste permanently deleted when try-count reaches 0
sd-item4 = Failed decryption attempts count toward try-count
burn-after-title = Burn After Reading
burn-after-desc = When enabled, the paste is automatically deleted after the first successful decryption. This is enforced by a cryptographic receipt — the client proves it fully decrypted the content before the server deletes it.
ba-item1 = Paste deleted immediately after first successful view
ba-item2 = Cryptographic burn receipt proves decryption, no client trust required
ba-item3 = Falls back to TTL expiry if the client never sends the receipt
ba-item4 = Failed attempts still count toward the try-count limit
proof-safety = Proof of Safety
proof-safety-desc = Your data's safety is guaranteed by end-to-end encryption. The server only stores encrypted chunks, the wrapped content key, and an HMAC of the validation key. Even a full server compromise cannot expose plaintext.
ps-item1 = Even server compromise cannot expose your plaintext data
ps-item2 = No backdoors — decryption keys exist only in your browser or CLI
ps-item3 = Self-destructing pastes minimize exposure window
ps-item4 = Failed attempts can be capped to prevent brute-force attacks
ps-item5 = Password changes never re-upload or re-encrypt your data
